From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Synkar <dev@synkar.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 19:45:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] rockchip: rkvenc: reject non-contiguous imported dma-bufs

rkvenc encodes with the IOMMU in passthrough (identity) mode: the
device default domain is IOMMU_DOMAIN_PASSTHROUGH, so dma_buf mappings
returned by dma_buf_map_attachment() are physical addresses and the
encoder hardware walks the physical range [buffer->iova, buffer->iova + size).

That is only safe when the imported dma-buf is physically contiguous
(single sg entry, e.g. CMA). For a page-scattered buffer (dma-buf heaps
"system" heap) the sg addresses are not sequential, and letting the
hardware write the whole reported range scribbles over arbitrary RAM,
producing random kernel crashes far from the offending code.

Verify the sg entries of an imported buffer are physically contiguous
(each sg_dma_address() equals the previous entry address plus length)
and reject the import otherwise. A rejected import fails the encode with
a clear error instead of silently corrupting memory.

Signed-off-by: Synkar <dev@synkar.com>
---
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_iommu.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_iommu.c
@@ -187,9 +187,19 @@
 	{
 		struct scatterlist *sg;
 		size_t size = 0;
-
-		for (sg = sgt->sgl; sg; sg = sg_next(sg))
+		dma_addr_t addr, expect = sg_dma_address(sgt->sgl);
+
+		for (sg = sgt->sgl; sg; sg = sg_next(sg)) {
+			addr = sg_dma_address(sg);
+			if (addr != expect) {
+				rkvenc_err("fd %d not physically contiguous "
+					   "(%pad != %pad)\n", fd, &expect, &addr);
+				ret = -EINVAL;
+				goto fail_map;
+			}
 			size += sg_dma_len(sg);
+			expect += sg_dma_len(sg);
+		}
 		buffer->size = size;
 	}
 	buffer->attach = attach;
